2 CM AGL. GP 30 CM NORTH NEAR THE TOP OF A SPARSLEY COVERED RIDGE
</groundspeak:encoded_hints>
      <groundspeak:logs>
        <groundspeak:log id="772517233">
          <groundspeak:date>2018-05-21T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="5323897">brendan714</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP535 Abraham Lake

Posted:  N 52° 19.006 W 116° 20.627

My GPSr:  N 52° 19.008 W 116° 20.630&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since we had a little bit of extra time on our way home on holiday Monday of the May long weekend, we decided to make a few detours along the way.  This was one of them.  And WOW what a spot!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We parked as far as I dared take the Corolla then walked over from there.  A friendly fellow on a quad offered us a ride, but we were okay walking.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We followed the steep trail up to a magnificent viewpoint and [FOUND] this cap right nearby.  Wow, awesome!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Another amazing spot that I wouldn't have visited had it not been for the brass cap!  Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="682066566">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-04-29T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="2164408">indianajoan&amp;marcus</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP535-Abraham Lake

We were able to drive to within 125m and hiked up the quad trail. Got the cap and the nearby cache. Incredible view. TFTC&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="614661455">
          <groundspeak:date>2016-07-03T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="3854161">Stonagal</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP535 - Abraham Lake

N52° 19.006  W116° 20.627&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My wife and I hiked up the hill (as if there is only one!) overlooking Abraham Lake to find GCAAD4. As we descended I spotted this survey marker and decided to check it against the BCP database to date. Sure enough it was featured back in 2011. Bonus! This is new ground for me, as I've never logged a cache twice.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="552183164">
          <groundspeak:date>2015-10-24T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="836983">Coolcat21</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP535 Abraham Lake&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;((Posted: N52° 19.006 W116° 20.627

My GPSr: N52° 19.006 W116° 20.62[b]6[/b]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I walked a little further than I had to to get to this cap and the nicely seasoned cache that's hidden near by, but, really, as far as I'm concerned, that's part of the point of capping and caching in the first place. It was a glorious afternoon weather-wise and the walk up the small incline to this one was fun. Although I managed to walk right past the cap once, I also managed to find it on the my second pass and chuckled to myself, wondering how it was I didn't actually trip over it on the first pass.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This has got to be one of the best cap locations I've been to and I thank you very much OFTH for luring me out to this memorable spot on the earth!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This entry was edited by Coolcat21 on Tuesday, 27 October 2015 at 18:44:21 UTC.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="537332096">
          <groundspeak:date>2015-07-26T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="12776479">2Sloths</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP535 Abraham Lake

N52° 19.006 W116° 20.627&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Took some pictures at the cliffs near this cap, I didn't realize how high the cliff was and quickly backed off from the edge. Lady sloth scared me by sitting near the edge admiring the lake. Thanks for the cap.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
